Raw pointers to value types
===========================

`*T` is the raw, unmanaged pointer mode — no refcount, no
auto-free. `&v` takes the address of a value (struct or
scalar). `*p` dereferences. Through `*p`, the caller's
backing storage is shared: mutating `p.field` (with implicit
deref for `*Struct`) mutates the original. This is the
explicit aliasing mode — the opposite of value semantics.

Used heavily in kernel and OS code (`oskit/`) where ARC
overhead is unacceptable. Must round-trip cleanly across
all backends.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct PtvPoint
        x: int
        y: int

Address-of and dereference round-trip
-------------------------------------

`&v` produces `*T` pointing at v. `*p` dereferences. Writing
through the pointer is visible at the source.

    #test
    test_ptr_to_struct_aliases() -> unit
        var v = PtvPoint(3, 4)
        var p: *PtvPoint = &v
        p.x = 99                 // through-pointer write, implicit deref on *PtvPoint
        assert(v.x == 99, s"v.x should be 99 after p.x=99, got ${v.x}")
        assert(v.y == 4,  s"v.y should still be 4, got ${v.y}")

    #test
    test_ptr_round_trip_scalar() -> unit
        var n: int = 42
        var p: *int = &n
        *p = 100                 // explicit deref-assign for scalars
        assert(n == 100, s"n should be 100 through *p, got $n")
        n = 7
        assert(*p == 7,  s"*p should read 7 after n=7, got ${*p}")

PtvPointer parameter mutates caller's value
----------------------------------------

A `*T` parameter is the explicit "callee modifies caller's
data" pattern. Distinct from `f(v: T)` (which gets a local
copy per the value-struct rules).

    #test
    test_ptr_param_mutates_caller() -> unit
        var v = PtvPoint(1, 2)
        bump_through_ptr(&v)
        assert(v.x == 101, s"v.x should be 101 after bump_through_ptr, got ${v.x}")
        assert(v.y == 102, s"v.y should be 102 after bump_through_ptr, got ${v.y}")

    bump_through_ptr(p: *PtvPoint) -> unit
        p.x = p.x + 100
        p.y = p.y + 100

PtvPointer reassignment
--------------------

A `*T` variable can be retargeted to a different value. The
old target is unaffected by the retarget (no refcount, no
free).

    #test
    test_ptr_retarget() -> unit
        var a = PtvPoint(1, 1)
        var b = PtvPoint(2, 2)
        var p: *PtvPoint = &a
        assert(p.x == 1, s"p.x should be 1 (pointing at a), got ${p.x}")
        p = &b
        assert(p.x == 2, s"p.x should be 2 (pointing at b), got ${p.x}")
        p.x = 99
        assert(b.x == 99, s"b.x should be 99 (mutated through retargeted p), got ${b.x}")
        assert(a.x == 1,  s"a.x should still be 1 (p no longer points at a), got ${a.x}")
